 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1075 17592186533499 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1075 17592186533525 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1075 17592186533646 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1076 17592186045493 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1077 "selection" 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1078 17592186533296 17592186045417] [17592186533963 1079 #uuid "f81d8766-b273-5742-81e1-135f6f4971d0" 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1068 17592186533887 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1069 "q-343529" 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1070 17592186045486 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[$\n{{field-1}}$]$" 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1072 #uuid "88fe2774-53cf-5c90-a007-5fb6a5d78b33" 17592186045417] [17592186534031 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-({x}^{2}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-(2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[\\frac{5}{x-2}-(2x+3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[\\frac{5-(x-2)(2x+3)}{x-2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[5-(x-2)(2x+3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[5-(2{x}^{2}-x-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{{e}^{{x}^{2}+3x}}[11+x-2{x}^{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1068 17592186533847 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1069 "q-138854" 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1070 17592186045486 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(g(x)-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1072 #uuid "cc13fb5c-5303-5c81-bafe-333779ced9ca" 17592186045417] [17592186534067 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{3/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}(\\frac{6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6x-({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+4)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=6x.\n$$" 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1068 17592186533784 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1069 "q-138832" 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1070 17592186045486 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}g(x)}{(x+8)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1072 #uuid "deb7ada4-14d9-5457-acd5-ef77477a4e36" 17592186045417] [17592186534079 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{4}+2x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{4}+2x-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+2x-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4{x}^{3}+2-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}(4{x}^{3}+2-\\frac{6}{x+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{6}}(\\frac{(4{x}^{3}+2)(x+8)-6}{x+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{7}}((4{x}^{3}+2)(x+8)-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}}{(x+8)^{7}}(4{x}^{4}+32{x}^{3}+2x+16-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{4}+2x}(2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+x+5)}{(x+8)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+x+5.\n$$" 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1068 17592186533963 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1069 "q-138834" 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1070 17592186045486 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x-1}}{(1-3x)^{6}}g(x)+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1072 #uuid "f3859b1c-a23b-517a-bfe5-a836f749be10" 17592186045417] [17592186534090 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-3x)^{5}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2x)-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-3x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{15}{1-3x})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{2{x}^{2}(1-3x)+15({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(1-3x)})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}(\\frac{17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15}{({x}^{2}+1)(1-3x)})+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x-1}}{(1-3x)^{6}}(17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15)+\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{x}}{(1-3x)^{5}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=17{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}+15.\n$$" 17592186045417]]}